'''6.''' An inflated speculation; a delusive commercial project, especially one which is put forward as insuring extraordinary profits; hence, a financial imposition or fraud; a cheating trick: as, the South Sea ''bubble.'' See below.
{{smaller block|This may not at first sight appear a large sum to those who remember the ''bubbles'' of 1825 and of 1845.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xxiv.}}}}
'''7†.''' A person deceived by an empty project; a dupe.
{{smaller block|He has been my ''bubble'' these twenty years.
{{right|''Arbuthnot'', John Bull.}}}}
{{smaller block|His pity and compassion make him sometimes a ''bubble'' to all his fellows. {{right|''Steele'', Tatler, No. 27.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Bubble Act''', an English statute of 1720, intended to restrict illusory schemes of corporate or associate organization: adopted to prevent the repetition of such frauds as the South Sea bubble.—'''Bubble and squeak.''' (''a'') A dish consisting of fried beef and cabbage: probably so called from the sounds made during frying.}}
