'''bubble'''<sup>1</sup> (bub′l), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''bubbled'', ppr. ''bubbling''. [= MLG. LG. ''bubbeln'' = MD. D. ''bobbelen'' = Dan. ''boble'', bubble; from the noun.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To rise in bubbles, as liquors when boiling or agitated; send up bubbles.—'''2.''' To run with a gurgling noise; gurgle: as, “''bubbling'' fountains,” ''Pope'', Autumn, l. 43.
{{smaller block|<poem>On yon swoll’n brook that ''bubbles'' fast
By meadows breathing of the past.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam, xcix.}}}}
'''3.''' To utter a bubbling or gurgling cry. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>At mine ear
''Bubbled'' the nightingale.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, iv.}}}}
'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To cause to bubble.
{{smaller block|I’d ''bubble'' up the water through a reed.
{{right|''Keats.''}}}}
'''2.''' To cheat; deceive or impose on; hoodwink; bamboozle.
{{smaller block|''Bubbled'' out of their goods and money!
{{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, i. 11.}}
When slavery could not bully, it ''bubbled'' its victim.
{{right|''W. Phillips'', Speeches, p. 377.}}}}

